@page "/utility-safe-area"

<CatHead Title="Safe area utilities">
    Three classes that hold an element of your own clear of the part of the screen the device has
    taken — a home indicator, a notch, a rounded corner.
</CatHead>

<div class="cat-note">
    <i class="ri-stack-line"></i>
    <strong>The cascade layer is what makes these work.</strong> A utility is <code>(0,1,0)</code>
    and a component rule like <code>.table th, .table td</code> is <code>(0,1,1)</code>, so a utility
    loses on specificity alone. <code>sedna.utilities</code> sits after <code>sedna.paint</code>, so
    <code>.text-end</code> on a table cell wins regardless of it.
</div>

<div class="cat-note">
    <i class="ri-smartphone-line"></i>
    <strong>The host page has to opt in.</strong> Until its viewport meta carries
    <code>viewport-fit=cover</code>, a browser lays the page out inside the display's safe area and
    every inset reports <code>0px</code> on every device. See
    <a href="/getting-started">Getting started</a>, which also carries the three-step migration for an
    app that already has the old tag.
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.UtilitySafeArea.SafeArea" Title="Holding an element clear" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.sedna-safe-top</code>, <code>.sedna-safe-bottom</code> and
        <code>.sedna-safe-inline</code> are for an element the library cannot see: a sticky action bar,
        a footer, a bottom nav, a cookie banner. The frame already holds itself clear — the shell
        pads the top and both sides, and everything pinned to the bottom edge (the sheet, the toast
        stack, the FAB, the reconnect bar) pads itself.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>They add padding, not margin, and that is the point.</strong> The element's own
        background then paints into the strip the device has taken. A margin leaves a transparent band
        there instead, and under a dark bar on a light page that band is what people file bugs about.
        Put the class on the element carrying the background, not on a wrapper around it.
    </p>
    <p>
        Nothing has to detect a device. <code>env()</code> is the detection — every inset is
        <code>0px</code> where there is nothing in the way, so one class is right on a phone, a tablet
        and a desktop.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<div class="cat-note">
    <i class="ri-code-line"></i>
    <strong>Where a utility does not fit, read the tokens.</strong>
    <code>--safe-block-start</code>, <code>--safe-block-end</code>, <code>--safe-inline-start</code>
    and <code>--safe-inline-end</code> are on the <a href="/tokens">Tokens</a> page. Use those rather
    than <code>env()</code> directly: the inline pair is mirrored for <code>dir="rtl"</code>, which
    <code>env()</code> cannot do on its own, and a token can be set to a value in a test where
    <code>env()</code> reports <code>0px</code> in every browser a test can drive.
</div>
